# Generated from blackflag-base.spec.in by stages/60-seed.sh -- do not edit the
# generated copy.  The PROVIDES marker below is replaced with the output of
# packaging/gen-base-provides.sh, which reads the live system.  (The marker token is
# spelled out only once below, so that this comment does not get substituted too.)
#
# This package intentionally owns NO files.  It is installed with `rpm -i --justdb`
# so that the rpm database reflects the LFS userland that was built before rpm
# existed.  See docs/ARCHITECTURE.md section 5 for the trade-offs and the plan to
# retire it.
Name:           blackflag-base
Version:        1.0.0
Release:        1%{?dist}
Summary:        Virtual package describing the pre-RPM BlackFlag base system

License:        MIT
URL:            https://blackflag.com.bd
BuildArch:      x86_64

Requires:       blackflag-release

@PROVIDES@

%description
BlackFlag Linux was bootstrapped from Linux From Scratch, so its base userland --
glibc, bash, coreutils, systemd, the toolchain and the core libraries -- was
installed before any package manager existed and is therefore owned by no RPM.

This package owns no files. It exists so that the RPM database can honestly answer
"is glibc present?" and so that dependency resolution for newly built BlackFlag
packages succeeds instead of trying to reinstall the running system. Its Provides
list is generated on the build host from the actual shared-library sonames,
pkg-config modules and base package versions found there.

As base components are rebuilt as real RPMs, they are removed from this list; when
it is empty this package is retired.

%files

